"[2511.09232v1] POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Low Resource Speech-to-Text Translation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09232v1)\nciteturn5952view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09232v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 31 Mar 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Low Resource Speech-to-Text Translation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xuanchen Li , cite7†Chenrui Cui , cite8†Tianrui Wang , cite9†Meng Ge , cite10†Zikang Huang , cite11†Jin Li , cite12†Yizhou Peng , cite13†Longbiao Wang , cite14†Jianwu Dang , cite15†Nyima Tashi L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Low Resource Speech-to-Text Translation, by Xuanchen Li and 9 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Speech Large Language Models (SpeechLLMs) have achieved breakthroughs in multilingual speech-to-text translation (S2TT). However, existing approaches often overlook semantic commonalities across source languages, leading to biased translation performance. In this work, we propose \\textbf{POTSA} (Parallel Optimal Transport for Speech Alignment), a new framework based on cross-lingual parallel speech pairs and Optimal Transport (OT), designed to bridge high- and low-resource translation gaps.\nL17: First, we introduce a Bias Compensation module to coarsely align initial speech representations across languages. Second, we impose token-level OT constraints on a Q-Former using parallel speech pairs to establish fine-grained consistency of representations. Then, we apply a layer scheduling strategy to focus OT constraints on the most semantically beneficial layers.\nL18: Experiments on the FLEURS dataset show that our method achieves SOTA performance, with +0.93 BLEU on average over five common languages and +5.05 BLEU on zero-shot languages, using only 10 hours of parallel speech per source language.\nL19: Comments:  | 5 pages, 3 figures, submitted to ICASSP 2026\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Sound (cs.SD)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite18†arXiv:2511.09232 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite19†arXiv:2511.09232v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09232†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Xuanchen Li [cite21†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 11:47:56 UTC (509 KB)\nL28: cite22†[v2] Mon, 30 Mar 2026 07:44:23 UTC (520 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v3] Tue, 31 Mar 2026 01:39:46 UTC (520 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled POTSA: A Cross-Lingual Speech Alignment Framework for Low Resource Speech-to-Text Translation, by Xuanchen Li and 9 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite16†View PDF L38:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite23†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite24†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite25†< prev |   cite26†next > L47: \nL48: cite27†new | cite28†recent | cite29†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite30†cs L53: cite31†cs.SD L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite32†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite33†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite34†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citat