3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 16 Nov 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:C$^3$TG: Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yu Li , cite7†Zhe Yang , cite8†Yi Huang , cite9†Xin Liu , cite10†Guilin Qi L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled C$^3$TG: Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation, by Yu Li and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable text generation capabilities. However, controlling specific attributes of generated text remains challenging without architectural modifications or extensive fine-tuning. Current methods typically toggle a single, basic attribute but struggle with precise multi-attribute control.\nL17: In scenarios where attribute requirements conflict, existing methods lack coordination mechanisms, causing interference between desired attributes. Furthermore, these methods fail to incorporate iterative optimization processes in the controlled generation pipeline. To address these limitations, we propose Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation (C$^3$TG), a two-phase framework for fine-grained, multi-dimensional text attribute control.\nL18: During generation, C$^3$TG selectively pairs the LLM with the required attribute classifiers from the 17 available dimensions and employs weighted KL-divergence to adjust token probabilities. The optimization phase then leverages an energy function combining classifier scores and penalty terms to resolve attribute conflicts through iterative feedback, enabling precise control over multiple dimensions simultaneously while preserving natural text flow.\nL19: Experiments show that C$^3$TG significantly outperforms baselines across multiple metrics including attribute accuracy, linguistic fluency, and output diversity, while simultaneously reducing toxicity. These results establish C$^3$TG as an effective and flexible solution for multi-dimensional text attribute control that requires no costly model modifications.\nL20: Comments:  | This paper has been accepted as a poster presentation at AAAI-2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.09292 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.09292v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09292†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Yu Li [cite16†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 12:59:23 UTC (2,142 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Sun, 16 Nov 2025 14:19:40 UTC (2,142 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled C$^3$TG: Conflict-aware, Composite, and Collaborative Controlled Text Generation, by Yu Li and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite11†View PDF L38:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite17†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite18†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite24†cs L53: cite25†cs.AI L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data pr