 ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:LiteraryTaste: A Preference Dataset for Creative Writing Personalization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†John Joon Young Chung , cite6†Vishakh Padmakumar , cite7†Melissa Roemmele , cite8†Yi Wang , cite9†Yuqian Sun , cite10†Tiffany Wang , cite11†Shm Garanganao Almeda , cite12†Brett A. Halperin , cite13†Yuwen Lu , cite14†Max Kreminski L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled LiteraryTaste: A Preference Dataset for Creative Writing Personalization, by John Joon Young Chung and 9 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF cite16†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:People have different creative writing preferences, and large language models (LLMs) for these tasks can benefit from adapting to each user's preferences. However, these models are often trained over a dataset that considers varying personal tastes as a monolith.\nL17: To facilitate developing personalized creative writing LLMs, we introduce LiteraryTaste, a dataset of reading preferences from 60 people, where each person: 1) self-reported their reading habits and tastes (stated preference), and 2) annotated their preferences over 100 pairs of short creative writing texts (revealed preference).\nL18: With our dataset, we found that: 1) people diverge on creative writing preferences, 2) finetuning a transformer encoder could achieve 75.8% and 67.7% accuracy when modeling personal and collective revealed preferences, and 3) stated preferences had limited utility in modeling revealed preferences. With an LLM-driven interpretability pipeline, we analyzed how people's preferences vary. We hope our work serves as a cornerstone for personalizing creative writing technologies.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2511.09310 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2511.09310v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09310†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: John Chung [cite20†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 13:21:33 UTC (545 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled LiteraryTaste: A Preference Dataset for Creative Writing Personalization, by John Joon Young Chung and 9 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite15†View PDF L35:   * cite16†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite21†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite22†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L44: \nL45: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2025-11 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite28†cs L50: cite29†cs.HC L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite32†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL74: \nL75: Bibliographic Explorer (cite33†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Connected Papers (cite34†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Litmaps (cite35†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL84: \nL85: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL86: \nL87: scite Smart Citations (cite36†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL88: \nL89: [Input] Code, Data, Media\nL90: # Code